INT. VILLAGE SCHOOLHOUSE KITCHEN, KARAKORAM FOOTHILLS - EARLY MORNING

Mud-brick walls. A copper kettle. Pale light through a slit window, catching flour in the air like small stars.

Five members of one family move around a low table. Bread is being made.

GRANDMOTHER ZAINAB QURESHI-ABBASOV (80s, headscarf the color of dried apricots) kneads dough with enormous patience.

Her daughter-in-law FARIDA QURESHI (50s, sleeves rolled) shapes loaves, one hand quick on the dough, one hand on the baby wrapped at her back.

Her granddaughter SAMINA QURESHI (17, exam papers poking from her satchel) lays out clay plates.

Her grandson TIMUR QURESHI (12, ink on his fingers) feeds the clay oven with dry twigs.

Her youngest, a girl of six called LAILA, lingers at the door, watching her feet.

ZAINAB
Samina, the salt.

Samina brings it. Zainab pinches some, crushes it between her fingers, adds it to the dough.

TIMUR
The oven's ready, Dadi.

ZAINAB
Is it? Show me.

He holds out his palm to the oven mouth. Holds it there. Yanks it back.

ZAINAB (CONT'D)
Ready.

Farida slides the first loaf onto the paddle. The smell rises, warm and sweet. Laila steps in.

LAILA
Can I help?

FARIDA
Of course.

She hands Laila a small ball of dough. Laila kneads it clumsily.

A knock at the door. Everyone looks up.

A stranger steps in, shaking snow from his shoulders: MR. ELIAS HARTMANN-DOGAN (40s, regional inspector, leather satchel, wet boots), blinking in the warm light.

ELIAS
Forgive me. I'm looking for the headmistress.

SAMINA
That's my mother.

FARIDA
(wiping her hands)
Mr. Hartmann-Dogan. We did not expect you until Thursday.

ELIAS
The road was clear.

ZAINAB
The road is never clear.

ELIAS
It was today.

Zainab studies him a long moment. Then cuts a heel of bread, hands it to him.

ZAINAB
Eat first. Papers later.

He bites. His shoulders drop.

ELIAS
This is extraordinary.

SAMINA
It's Dadi's.

ZAINAB
It is everyone's.

The baby coos. Laila giggles. Timur feeds the oven another twig. The kettle begins, finally, to sing.
